No problem. I'll ask Kate to do that.
Shall we start the meeting?
Yes. I think we're ready.
Let me start this meeting by saying welcome to the U.K. to our friends from Japan.
Thank you very much. Both Mr. Kogure and myself are very happy to be here in London to have a meeting with you who are one of our most important partners.
Thank you, Mr. Simizu. We at Brompton and Sons are also very glad to have you gentlemen visiting us in spite of your very busy schedule.
Thank you, Mrs. Mackin. I also have to say that our chairman Mr. Yukio Kosugi asked me to pass you his warmest regards to you all, particularly your chairman, Mr. James Thompson.
There's a rumor that I always give such a lengthy presentation that makes the audience feel sleepy. Of course that's wrong.
Is that so? Then, what will happen?
Very often it's I that doze off during the presentation due to jet fatigue.
In the beginning I have to thank you very much for the perfect program you have prepared for me.
You are welcome. Sorry we have failed to make arrangements for the weather. Having the rain at this time of the year is very unusual.
Would you update us now about the consumer behavior in Japan?
Do you mean that people in Japan are eager to buy the products which are just in fashion?
Correct. Through mass media they know what particular products and styles are trendy, and they just go shopping and get them.
In your opinion, can the average Japanese company employee afford buy such trendy goods as they want?
Yes. But they are very choosy in a way.
By choosy you mean that they are always economy-minded?
Not necessarily. They are very quality-conscious instead. If they find something that has good value for money, they don't hesitate to pay hundreds of thousands of yen for it.
In my opinion, the business conditions will get much better by the end of this year.
Really? Why is it that you think so?
Well, because we know the Government will introduce some more pump-priming measures.
Would you tell us how you evaluate this plan?
I think it's more realistic than what we had at our last meeting. It now identifies all the major factors and has completely discussed all the problems and opportunities before us.
May I ask you a couple of questions?
In the presentation you said about thirty-five percent of your turnover comes from your business with Asia, right?
Could you tell me how your Asian business performed last year and how it will be next year?
It's our understanding that you operate various types of shops in Europe. Could you tell me what types you have?
We have three types of retail outlets, and they are convenience stores, supermarkets, and hypermarkets.
What kind of store is the hypermarket?
It's a very large supermarket located outside a big city. It has ample car parking facilities.
Are you covered by product liability insurance? Just in case, we may have to claim a damage on the goods you supply.
Of course we are. Not only our finished products but the packing materials we use are also covered separately.
Could you share half the promotional cost in the initial year as all our suppliers do?
I don't think it's impossible. We know it's important and we'd be willing to discuss it once we have your reasonable marketing plan.
Is it true that the department store is threatened by supermarkets and discount houses in Japan?
Not necessarily so. I think they have learned to coexist after many years of competition.
Do the consumers in Japan usually buy food at local supermarkets?
Yes. But in smaller cities and even in the old downtown area of Tokyo, we have a lot of small independent specialized retail stores.
In case we have plans to open a hypermarket chain in Japan, could we place our orders directly with major manufacturers?
I don't think so. You'd have to do business with some of the major wholesalers.
May I ask you why?
No particular reason. It's just custom. You'd have to book your order with a wholesaler even if the goods come directly from your manufacturer.
Most Japanese business companies normally follow two ways to find new employees, recruiting from schools and advertising in print media. It seems that the latter is often very effective.
Do Japanese take long holidays in summer?
Schools are closed for five-six weeks. Government offices remain open but their employees take several days off by turns. Businesses do about the same way, thought some companies are closed for a week or so.
Let me confirm that you have agreed to amend our current contract to allow us more time to work out a sales plan for the following year, right?
Exactly. I hope you are happy.
I think we are. Now we have only one point yet to discuss. It's how many months of prior notice each party should give the other in case of termination of the contract.
That's right. We insist on six months while you say that you need one year to sell out your stock.
How do you like this idea? We accept your condition of six months' notice provided you grant us the right to sell for six months after termination of the contract.
Interesting. But in that case there could be competition between our product you sell and what we would ask your successor to sell.
I think that just wraps it up for today, gentlemen.
Sorry, but I think there's one thing we have to confirm before we leave. That's about your special discount you mentioned in your fax.
I'm very happy to see almost all the items on our agenda have been discussed and agreed on. Shall we leave for dinner?
Yes, thank you. May I ask you how soon you will let us know your decision about the remaining two items?
On behalf of Yamato Kogyo I must say thank you very much for your kindness and help in leading this meeting to end up with such good results.
I'm very pleased to hear that, Mr. Shirane. We're always willing to do our best to help you.
Especially I appreciate your acceptance of our A and P Plan for next year, positioned as the initial year of investment for the future.
I believe a meeting like this is a good opportunity for us to get to know and understand each other personally.
I share your opinion, Mr. Shirane. Mr. John White, our chairman, also places much importance on the same idea.
Before we call it a day, would you give me a few minutes?
It's been a great pleasure for me to visit your head offices and have substantial discussions with you. Thank you very much for your kind cooperation.
I'm very glad that we have been able to discuss all the important issues and to find solutions. I feel very much obliged to you for your kind collaboration.
My colleagues and I are happy to see that everything has been settled. Thank you very much.
